### Differentiating using the power rule

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}g'(x)
\\\\
&=\dfrac{d}{dx}\left(x^{-3}\right)
\\\\
&=-3x^{-3-1}\qquad\gray{\text{The power rule}}
\\\\
&=-3x^{-4}
\end{align}$

### Evaluating $g'(x)$

So we found that $g'(x)=-3x^{-4}$, which can also be written as $-\dfrac{3}{x^4}$.

Now let's plug $\blueD{x=3}$:

$\begin{align}
-\dfrac{3}{(\blueD{3})^4}&=-\dfrac{3}{81}
\\\\
&=-\dfrac{1}{27}
\end{align}$
